.TH HD-PIPES 6 "November 2026" "hd-screensavers" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-pipes \- the 3D pipes screensaver of HyprDebian
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-pipes
.RB [ \-\-joints
.IR elbow | ball | cycle ]
.RB [ \-\-cycles
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-delay
.IR us ]
.RB [ \-\-[no\-]fisheye ]
.RB [ \-\-[no\-]tightturns ]
.RB [ \-\-[no\-]rotatepipes ]
.RB [ \-\-framerate
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-msaa
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-window ]
.br
.B hd-pipes \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-pipes
draws the 3D pipes of Windows NT, a screensaver of the package
.BR hd-screensavers .
It is based on
.I hacks/glx/pipes.c
of xscreensaver, from xlockmore, by Marcelo F. Vianna, and has its grid
walk, its lights, materials and colours, and its options.
.PP
Pipes grow from cell to cell through a grid of 33\(mu25\(mu33 cells, one
step per
.BR \-\-delay ,
each pipe in one colour, turning at elbows or balls.
A pipe ends, capped with a ball, when it is boxed in or reaches
.BR \-\-size .
After
.B \-\-cycles
pipes, a screenful, the scene shrinks, tilts by 90\(de and starts again on
an empty grid.
.PP
It puts a surface on the overlay layer of every monitor, of
wlr-layer-shell, with the keyboard to itself, and follows the monitors that
come and go while it runs.
Every monitor has a scene of its own.
It ends on a key press, a move of the mouse, a click, a scroll or a touch,
and on SIGTERM or SIGINT.
The pointer that is on a monitor when it starts does not end it; the
cursor is hidden.
.PP
It draws with Vulkan, on the first discrete GPU that can show on the
compositor, else an integrated one, else a virtual one, else lavapipe of
mesa-vulkan-drivers, which renders on the CPU.
It needs Vulkan 1.3.
The shading is per pixel, with up to 8 samples per pixel
.RB ( \-\-msaa ),
and the frames come at the refresh rate of each monitor, or at most
.B \-\-framerate
a second; the pipes grow at the same speed at any frame rate.
.PP
.B hd-launch-screensaver
of
.B hd-desktop
starts it, without arguments, when the key
.B hd-screensaver
of the configuration names it, see
.BR hd-screensaver (7);
the package sets that key in the configuration of the system when it is
installed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-joints " \fIelbow\fR|\fIball\fR|\fIcycle"
The joints where the pipes turn.
.I cycle
takes balls and elbows by turns, one kind per screenful, balls first.
Default:
.IR cycle .
.TP
.BI \-\-cycles " N"
The pipes per screenful, 1 to 10.
Default: 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " N"
The longest run of a pipe, in steps, 10 to 1000.
Default: 500.
.TP
.BI \-\-delay " us"
The time of one step, in microseconds, 1 to 1000000.
Default: 10000.
.TP
.BR \-\-fisheye ", " \-\-no\-fisheye
The camera closer to the pipes, or further away.
Default: on.
.TP
.BR \-\-tightturns ", " \-\-no\-tightturns
With it, every step has the same chance of a turn; without it, the chance
grows with every step since the last turn.
Default: off.
.TP
.BR \-\-rotatepipes ", " \-\-no\-rotatepipes
The scene turns slowly about its vertical axis, or stands still.
Default: on.
.TP
.BI \-\-framerate " N"
The most frames per second on each monitor, 1 to 1000, or 0 for a frame at
every refresh.
Fewer frames take less of the GPU; the pipes grow at the same speed.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-msaa " N"
The most samples per pixel against jagged edges, 1 to 8.
It takes 1, 2, 4 or 8, the most the GPU has up to
.IR N ;
1 is no multisampling.
Fewer samples take less of the GPU.
Default: 8.
.TP
.B \-\-window
One ordinary window instead of every monitor, which input does not end;
Escape or closing the window ends it.
For trying the options on a desktop and for screenshots.
It is not read from the configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the options with their ranges and defaults, and exit.
.PP
Each option is
.BI \-\- name " value"
or
.BI \-\- name = value\fR.
When an option is given more than once, the last wins.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The key
.B hd-pipes
of the configuration sets the options too, as an object with their names:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"hd-pipes": {"joints": "ball", "cycles": 3, "fisheye": false}}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The flags are
.B true
or
.BR false ,
the numbers whole numbers and the joints a string.
It is read in the configuration of the system,
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
and then in that of the user,
.IR ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json ,
member by member: an option that the user leaves out keeps the value of
the system.
Run as root, it only reads the configuration of the system.
The command line wins over both.
See
.BR hd-config (7).
.PP
To set it for a user:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-config-update hd-pipes '{"joints": "ball"}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
A value out of its range or of the wrong type, a member that is not an
option, a key that is not an object and a file that is not valid JSON are
reported on stderr and skipped, so the value below stays.
On the command line a wrong value is an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
It ended, on input, on SIGTERM or SIGINT, or after
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
It cannot start: no Vulkan device, no wlr-layer-shell, no monitors; or it
lost the compositor.
The reason is on stderr.
.TP
.B 2
A wrong argument.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hyprdebian/config.json
The configuration of the user, with the key
.BR hd-pipes .
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The configuration of the system, with the keys
.B hd-pipes
and
.BR hd-screensaver .
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/hd-screensavers/copyright
The notice of
.IR pipes.c .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-screensaver (7),
.BR hd-config (7),
.BR hypridle (1)
